 disturbance  which is a threat to the forest  in addition  global warming now is proving to be a  bit of a problem because it appears to  have changed the climatic patterns a bit  which means that agricultural patterns  have had to change  crops that used to grow at a particular  elevational range don't do as well any  longer  we seem to be getting more intense  storms  more intense rainfall events uh which  have had  impacts um there is a change in the  flora and fauna and insects which which  never used to occur in fungal diseases  which never used to occur uh seem to be  occurring in some parts  india has for a long time had a huge  shortage of power  the  easiest supposedly greenest way to  generate more power is through  hydropower and there has been the  building of a large number of hydropower  projects across the himalaya many more  are still planned  however the himalaya are in a fairly  sesmically active zone it's it's called  a zone five which is the highest level  of sesame city um also the mountains are  relatively young they're they're well  under 60 million years old which in  geological terms is uh very very recent  they're still growing taller so it's  it's very youthful mountains and as a  result are extremely crumbly uh  situating large dams or more commonly  now what are called run of the river  projects which are basically large  pipelines which are bored through  mountains and and then give an adequate  head to generate hydropower  is extremely risky in such environments  if seismic activity occurs which it will  eventually  it could lead to problems in these  hydropower projects  hydropower is also resulting in changes  in the diversity  it is resulting in in wildlife corridors  being impacted there is a lot of  relocation of human beings which needs  to happen because as mountain systems go  the himalaya are fairly well populated  the himalaya has often been studied for  the north-south transects the very very  sudden changes in elevation from about  sea level to uh 8 000 meters or more in  a very very short distance however  what's equally interesting or more  interesting and what we are looking at  through the el himalaya initiative is  the east-west arc of the east west  transect  from afghanistan and pakistan in the  west to burma in the east  and along this gradient you have a large  variety of  environmental social and cultural  gradients you go from  dry desert-like conditions in the west  to the wettest spot on earth in  gerapunji in the eastern himalaya  you go from  a  very tropical forest with a very very  tropical trait even at mid elevational  zones to forests which have a much more  uh temperate uh trait you find the  entire spectrum of religions  most of the major religions of the  planet are well represented in the  himalayas it's it's unusual to see such  a small area with  all these various religions christianity  buddhism islam hinduism coexisting and  coexisting fairly well in many places  you have  very high endemism which is  species that are sort of unique to the  area among the western side of the  himalaya whereas on the east you have  extremely high biodiversity and it's  it's considered one of the biodiversity  hot spots of the earth  the himalaya is also  the  sort of land from where many major  rivers flow and 10 major river basins  get a lot of their water from the  himalayan region and the ecosystem  services that flow from the himalaya  impact about a fifth of humanity over a  billion people in the indo-gangetic  plains  in the plains of punjab and further  north in tibet and china  biodiversity species animals plants  don't recognize political borders and  they move whether the borders are there  or not what's interesting is that people  also don't recognize political borders  to the extent that we might think they  do  the indigenous groups there will often  cross borders which are thought to be  extremely hostile and disputed  without really thinking too much about  it  yes certainly  with  the  several of the countries  in the himalayan zone getting into  conflict borders have become a lot less  porous than they used to be  but the movement across borders still  occurs  both for the flora and fauna but also  for human beings and a certain amount of  trade that still goes on  in the u.s when we talk about forest  disturbance the focus is usually more on  what we call acute disturbance  clear cutting of forests or even  thinning of stands which results in the  removal of a fair number of trees  in the western himalaya in particular  the disturbance is different we call it  chronic disturbance  it is something that happens repeatedly  but is of a much lower intensity the  agro ecosystem which  which which forms the main  livelihood pattern of the local people  there  is very dependent on  these biomass products which would be a  wood which is not  obtained through the cutting of entire  trees but rather the lopping of branches  leaf litter which is obtained by  sweeping the forest floor clean off  leaves that fall once a year that's a  very interesting pattern of the  himalayan trees the western himalayan  trees most of them lose their leaves  after 12 months of of getting them and  almost all of them lose their leaves in  the spring in march and april and after  that  women from villages correct collect  these headloads of leaves and take them  out of the forest  fodder is also obtained largely from the  forest and interestingly enough in this  ecosystem  people lock the trees remove leaves from  high branches  and and take them to feed to their  cattle  uh whereas if you move from the western  part of the himalaya uh  cattle and buffalos  are really the main  livestock that are present there and  they are integral to the agricultural  systems in the past they were the only  source of uh draft power they were used  to plow the fields  you still cannot have much mechanization  on those slopes so you still need  ox when you want to plow your fields  the  interestingly the cattle dung that comes  from these  cows was used to form the main  fertilizer for the fields it was  composted along with the leaf litter and  that was the main nutrient input  but as we go east  you moved into a different kind of  agricultural practice you had shifting  agriculture or slash and burn  agriculture as it's known where the  trees would all be cut down either  stubble would be burnt  the area would be cultivated for two or  three years and then the ecosystem would  be left to recover for a period of five  to fifteen or twenty years  in those areas the importance of cattle  is much less  but meat becomes extremely important and  piggery is is the main  livestock that you find in the eastern  himalayan  biogas is an extremely interesting  technology which hasn't got the  importance that it should be getting in  the western himalaya  we  carry out afforestation programs there  with the hope that the forest will come  back  but unless the local people there have  an option have alternatives to firewood  you cannot have forests coming back  liquid petroleum gas which is used along  the road head is not very practical to  get into the interior parts of the  himalaya and biogas forms an extremely  important and interesting alternative  to fuel wood  what happens with biogas is that the  dung or cow dung is mixed with water  made into a slurry  put into a large chamber which is  airtight which is built for this purpose  and left to ferment  methane gas is released  which forms an extremely clean fuel  which can be used for cooking  the residue that is left the slurry  which which is uh  reduced in carbon content which comes  out as methane gas  still has all the nutrients in it so it  makes an extremely effective fertilizer  all the pathogens and and nematodes and  other things uh in the slurry are killed  through the biogas process so it's a  it's a very good fertilizer more  nutritious and and with much less in  terms of uh disease  the other good thing with biogas is that  it's a clean fuel  at present there's a lot of attention  that's been focused to what we call  black carbon small residues particles of  carbon which get into the upper  atmosphere largely as a result of  biomass burning  burning  dirty fuel woods which have moisture in  them in particular and this black carbon  gets into the upper atmospheric layers  it forms a haze it gets deposited on the  glaciers in the himalaya which increases  their the ability of the glaciers to  absorb sunlight and  increases the rate of melt of glacier so  it's a really serious problem when you  use biogas you don't get black carbon to  that extent so again it's it's better  for the environment it's better  also of course for the local villagers  who use it because the respiratory and  eye ailments which tend to be very very  high amongst women who cook on wood  fuels  is much much lower when they're using  bio gas  however biogas has not found  the kind of acceptability  or has not spread as well as we would  have hoped the major issue we see with  biogas is the high up front cost um a  one to two cubic meter biogas plant  which is what would be required for a  for a typical family which would be five  or six people would cost about 10 to 15  000 rupees or about 300  or so  to construct  this represents a fairly large amount of  money for  rural people and it is one reason why it  doesn't get built as often as it should  however the savings from biogas in terms  of  carbon sequestration that occurs when  you're not burning so much wood in terms  of impacts on health in terms of impact  on on overall global warming and black  carbon would be worth a lot more than  three hundred dollars so this would be  what i would call a good subsidy even if  we have to  pay people  most of the amount they need to build a  biogas plant it would be well worth it  a few other problems with biogas also  include it requires a little bit of  water so where acute water shortages  occur it doesn't work that well and you  need some trained technicians so while  the level of engineering expertise is  very very basic to keep a biogas plant  functional if people don't have that you  often have the pipelines getting clogged  with a little bit of water maybe in and  just because people don't know how to  drain it  it ceases to function  the himalayan ecosystem is also  extremely interesting to study for  things like chronic disturbance or for  for other kinds of studies  because the uh flora the the large trees  there  are relatively  lacking in diversity which makes it easy  to model this ecosystem for example  we have mainly two species at the mid  elevational range which exists over much  of the himalaya  these are the oaks and the pines and you  have a few species of oak and largely  one species of pine  human disturbance tends to  increase the distribution of pine over  oak because oak has a fuel wood which  has extremely high calorific value so  it's preferred for burning the leaves of  oak  are relatively nutritious it's not a  great fodder but it's the most available  fodder so the tree tends to be lopped  by humans and finally the leaf litter is  extremely nutritious again and and forms  a much better compost than most other  kinds of trees  in most trees when the leaf is about to  fall there's a high re-translocation of  nutrients back into the tree and the  leaf that fall actually has fairly low  levels of nitrogen and phosphorus and  other nutrients  in  quercus leucotrichophora and other  himalayan oaks there is a fairly high  level of nutrients that is left in the  leaves in other words the reed  translocation is low what this does is  that the leaves that fall onto the  forest floor are rich in nutrients they  rot more easily  and the nutrients goes back into the  forest soils and then can be reabsorbed  by the trees so you have a external mode  of  nutrient movement rather than than the  tree just  bringing the leaves back but as a result  of its utility to the local people  banjok or the other oaks in the himalaya  tend to be locked much more  they are more susceptible to fire in  that  seedlings tend to be damaged more than  the seedlings of pine would be damaged  they tend to be grazed more so when  cattle are left in the forest oak  seedlings tend to be impacted more and  all of these together result in the  distribution of pine visory oak  increasing  which actually is converse to what  normally happens you normally have oak  taking over from pine but because of  human disturbance pine often takes over  from oak in the himalaya  so when we think of the himalaya we  think of these high snow-capped  mountains and rightly so  pretty much all the highest peaks in the  world are in the himalaya  but when we actually  look at a section of the himalaya it's  not all snow-capped peaks  the himalaya rises fairly suddenly to  about 2000 meters  but after that it stays at about that  elevation for some 150 kilometers or so  and varies from 1500 to maybe two and a  half thousand meters after which it  suddenly rises up to six or seven  thousand meters  now what this does is is something  interesting in that the there is a broad  expanse of land about 150 kilometers in  depth in the southern himalaya which is  extremely suitable for  human settlements as well as forests  these forests have extremely high  productivity this is the zone of oak and  pine  mixed with some amount of older and  poplar and maples  actually not very different from the  kind of ecosystem in terms of species  that you would expect in in the  northeast us but very very different  species endemic species that have  extremely different characteristics  the oaks for example are evergreen uh  they're not deciduous uh they don't form  very good rings the pines have a lower  leaf lifespan than in the pines you  would see in the u.s  the needles uh fall after about 12  months or so giving the pine  almost deciduous kind of characteristic  and and in the spring you'll see the  pine trees with relatively  few needles as the old needles fall and  the new ones have not yet fully expanded  so you have this very broad expanse of  the himalaya which has high productivity  high settlements  a fairly dense population and this is  the zone that that makes for  a lot of interesting study whether you  are a sociologist an anthropologist an  ecologist  or just  looking at an integration of all of  these things  most of the trees in the himalayan  ecosystem  lose their leaves in the spring  and these leaves actually turn out to be  an extremely valuable resource for human  beings  the oak leaves for example are used to  make a compost fertilizer after mixing  them with cow dung and this is the main  nutrient input for himalayan agriculture  in the western himalaya or central  himalaya  the pine leaves are also fairly useful  they're used for packing of the  horticultural fruit  so packing of apples they're also being  used for briquetting now to produce  energy  but all of this has a fairly  significant impact on the ecosystem  which is not extremely well studied and  not very well thought out  as well government policies do not  condone in any way the removal of leaf  litter and in fact encourage the removal  of pine litter because it is thought to  cause the ground fires  at cedar we've started looking at the  impacts of leaflet removal and and  they're fairly significant  of course soil compaction occurs so you  have a more dense  more compacted soil where germination of  species is is impacted  the  soil biota gets extremely impacted from  mycorrhizae  mycorrhizae are these associations  between beneficial associations between  fungi and plant roots which help  increase the volume of soil that can be  accessed by the plant roots and our  studies found that 95 percent or more  decrease in mycorrhizae in areas where  leaf litter removal was very common  the fine roots in the upper layers  decrease  the soil carbon decreases  the soil is also a huge repository of  carbon and in the surficial layers in a  good oak forest you can have more than  three percent carbon but once the forest  gets a bit degraded leaf litter is  removed for many many years  the soil carbon levels fall to under one  percent  um now this has huge impacts on on what  kind of species will generate there  regenerate them it also has a lot of uh  impact on the diversity of uh the ground  layer the herbaceous layer that exists  there as well as  one thing which again hasn't been  studied very well at all is the soil  biota the nematodes the earthworms the  various kinds of  small critters that live in the soil  layers and these of course are extremely  impacted  the himalaya by definition almost  have problems to start with  it's it's like an adolescent kid when  when  you know you're 15 years old you tend to  have more than your fair share of  problems and i'd say it's similar with  the himalaya they're a young and growing  mountain range  which is uh very fragile where many of  the rocks uh tend to be very crumbly uh  where seismic activity is already very  high um so it's it's not a very stable  mountain range um such as the mountain  ranges such as the alps for example  which tend to be an old and stable  mountain range  the high population that has been living  there has resulted in the fragmentation  of the natural areas and as global  warming occurs and global warming  interestingly enough seems to be  occurring faster or at a at a more  accentuated rate in the himalaya the  glaciers seem to be melting a little  faster  the  climate change across the slope seems to  be higher  it is impacting the way the natural  flora and fauna can move especially now  that human beings have sort of taken  over patches of the landscape and the  corridors for migration of that flora  and fauna have been greatly cut down  so the himalaya faces threats on several  fronts  another major one is the development  activity  we all want electricity and so  hydropower needs to be built  but is hydropower are large dams or even  run of the river dams appropriate for  the himalaya  there's a lot of road building activity  going on without proper alignment once  you disturb a fragile rock  it's often very very hard to stabilize  it and roads that are built incorrectly  will result in land slips and landslides  that go on for years to come so there  are problems on on many different levels  in the himalaya  the himalaya makes an extremely  interesting research area  and in some ways it's easy to study the  tree diversity for example in the  western himalayas relatively low  but what makes it so interesting and  complex  is the interplay of  nature geology ecology forestry and  human beings  unlike other forested ecosystems in the  world you don't have large swaths of  forest and then  you know human populations on the  periphery of that  human beings integrate very very well  with the natural areas they're dependent  on the natural areas of these forests  and pretty much all the forests are  impacted by human beings in one way or  the other studying this interplay of  humans and nature and wildlife  is extremely interesting  the himalayas also been called the third  pole  in addition to the north and south pole  why do we call the himalaya pole well  that's because they've also been called  the water towers of asia that's because  there's an amazing amount of snow and  ice and glaciers that exist in the  himalaya this is very important  implications for the world if global  warming  makes those glaciers melt faster than  glaciers elsewhere  it is going to have implications not  just for global climate not just for  changes in albedo and reflectivity but  for the 1 billion people or more that  live in the indo-gangetic and other  major basins of the himalaya  but the  point is here that the study of these  glaciers or other aspects of the  himalayan ecosystem is much lower than  what we would have liked  a recent ipcc report called the himalaya  a white spot because of the paucity of  research that exists on many areas so  this is an ecosystem that really  requires a lot more attention and a lot  more research

