where acute water shortages  occur it doesn't work that well and you  need some trained technicians so while  the level of engineering expertise is  very very basic to keep a biogas plant  functional if people don't have that you  often have the pipelines getting clogged  with a little bit of water maybe in and  just because people don't know how to  drain it  it ceases to function  the himalayan ecosystem is also  extremely interesting to study for  things like chronic disturbance or for  for other kinds of studies  because the uh flora the the large trees  there  are relatively  lacking in diversity which makes it easy  to model this ecosystem for example  we have mainly two species at the mid  elevational range which exists over much  of the himalaya  these are the oaks and the pines and you  have a few species of oak and largely  one species of pine  human disturbance tends to  increase the distribution of pine over  oak because oak has a fuel wood which  has extremely high calorific value so  it's preferred for burning the leaves of  oak  are relatively nutritious it's not a  great fodder but it's the most available  fodder so the tree tends to be lopped  by humans and finally the leaf litter is  extremely nutritious again and and forms  a much better compost than most other  kinds of trees  in most trees when the leaf is about to  fall there's a high re-translocation of  nutrients back into the tree and the  leaf that fall actually has fairly low  levels of nitrogen and phosphorus and  other nutrients  in  quercus leucotrichophora and other  himalayan oaks there is a fairly high  level of nutrients that is left in the  leaves in other words the reed  translocation is low what this does is  that the leaves that fall onto the  forest floor are rich in nutrients they  rot more easily  and the nutrients goes back into the  forest soils and then can be reabsorbed  by the trees so you have a external mode  of  nutrient movement rather than than the  tree just  bringing the leaves back but as a result  of its utility to the local people  banjok or the other oaks in the himalaya  tend to be locked much more  they are more susceptible to fire in  that  seedlings tend to be damaged more than  the seedlings of pine would be damaged  they tend to be grazed more so when  cattle are left in the forest oak  seedlings tend to be impacted more and  all of these together result in the  distribution of pine visory oak  increasing  which actually is converse to what  normally happens you normally have oak  taking over from pine but because of  human disturbance pine often takes over  from oak in the himalaya  so when we think of the himalaya we  think of these high snow-capped  mountains and rightly so  pretty much all the highest peaks in the  world are in the himalaya  but when we actually  look at a section of the himalaya it's  not all snow-capped peaks  the himalaya rises fairly suddenly to  about 2000 meters  but after that it stays at about that  elevation for some 150 kilometers or so  and varies from 1500 to maybe two and a  half thousand meters after which it  suddenly rises up to six or seven  thousand meters  now what this does is is something  interesting in that the there is a broad  expanse of land about 150 kilometers in  depth in the southern himalaya which is  extremely suitable for  human settlements as well as forests  these forests have extremely high  productivity this is the zone of oak and  pine  mixed with some amount of older and  poplar and maples  actually not very different from the  kind of ecosystem in terms of species  that you would expect in in the  northeast us but very very different  species endemic species that have  extremely different characteristics  the oaks for example are evergreen uh  they're not deciduous uh they don't form  very good rings the pines have a lower  leaf lifespan than in the pines you  would see in the u.s  the needles uh fall after about 12  months or so giving the pine  almost deciduous kind of characteristic  and and in the spring you'll see the  pine trees with relatively  few needles as the old needles fall and  the new ones have not yet fully expanded  so you have this very broad expanse of  the himalaya which has high productivity  high settlements  a fairly dense population and this is  the zone that that makes for  a lot of interesting study whether you  are a sociologist an anthropologist an  ecologist  or just  looking at an integration of all of  these things  most of the trees in the himalayan  ecosystem  lose their leaves in the spring  and these leaves actually turn out to be  an extremely valuable resource for human  beings  the oak leaves for example are used to  make a compost fertilizer after mixing  them with cow dung and this is the main  nutrient input for himalayan agriculture  in the western himalaya or central  himalaya  the pine leaves are also fairly useful  they're used for packing of the  horticultural fruit  so packing of apples they're also being  used for briquetting now to produce  energy  but all of this has a fairly  significant impact on the ecosystem  which is not extremely well studied and  not very well thought out  as well government policies do not  condone in any way the removal of leaf  litter and in fact encourage the removal  of pine litter because it is thought to  cause the ground fires  at cedar we've started looking at the  impacts of leaflet removal and and  they're fairly significant  of course soil compaction occurs so you  have a more dense  more compacted soil where germination of  species is is impacted  the  soil biota gets extremely impacted from  mycorrhizae  mycorrhizae are these associations  between beneficial associations between  fungi and plant roots which help  increase the volume of soil that can be  accessed by the plant roots and our  studies found that 95 percent or more  decrease in mycorrhizae in areas where  leaf litter removal was very common  the fine roots in the upper layers  decrease  the soil carbon decreases  the soil is also a huge repository of  carbon and in the surficial layers in a  good oak forest you can have more than  three percent carbon but once the forest  gets a bit degraded leaf litter is  removed for many many years  the soil carbon levels fall to under one  percent  um now this has huge impacts on on what  kind of species will generate there  regenerate them it also has a lot of uh  impact on the diversity of uh the ground  layer the herbaceous layer that exists  there as well as  one thing which again hasn't been  studied very well at all is the soil  biota the nematodes the earthworms the  various kinds of  small critters that live in the soil  layers and these of course are extremely  impacted  the himalaya by definition almost  have problems to start with  it's it's like an adolescent kid when  when  you know you're 15 years old you tend to  have more than your fair share of  problems and i'd say it's similar with  the himalaya they're a young and growing  mountain range  which is uh very fragile where many of  the rocks uh tend to be very crumbly uh  where seismic activity is already very  high um so it's it's not a very stable  mountain range um such as the mountain  ranges such as the alps for example  which tend to be an old and stable  mountain range  the high population that has been living  there has resulted in the fragmentation  of the natural areas and as global  warming occurs and global warming  interestingly enough seems to be  occurring faster or at a at a more  accentuated rate in the himalaya the  glaciers seem to be melting a little  faster  the  climate change across the slope seems to  be higher  it is impacting the way the natural  flora and fauna can move especially now  that human beings have sort of taken  over patches of the landscape and the  corridors for migration of that flora  and fauna have been greatly cut down  so the himalaya faces threats on several  fronts  another major one is the development  activity  we all want electricity and so  hydropower needs to be built  but is hydropower are large dams or even  run of the river dams appropriate for  the himalaya  there's a lot of road building activity  going on without proper alignment once  you disturb a fragile rock  it's often very very hard to stabilize  it and roads that are built incorrectly  will result in land slips and landslides  that go on for years to come so there  are problems on on many different levels  in the himalaya  the himalaya makes an extremely  interesting research area  and in some ways it's easy to study the  tree diversity for example in the  western himalayas relatively low  but what makes it so interesting and  complex  is the interplay of  nature geology ecology forestry and  human beings  unlike other forested ecosystems in the  world you don't have large swaths of  forest and then  you know human populations on the  periphery of that  human beings integrate very very well  with the natural areas they're dependent  on the natural areas of these forests  and pretty much all the forests are  impacted by human beings in one way or  the other studying this interplay of  humans and nature and wildlife  is extremely interesting  the himalayas also been called the third  pole  in addition to the north and south pole  why do we call the himalaya pole well  that's because they've also been called  the water towers of asia that's because  there's an amazing amount of snow and  ice and glaciers that exist in the  himalaya this is very important  implications for the world if global  warming  makes those glaciers melt faster than  glaciers elsewhere  it is going to have implications not  just for global climate not just for  changes in albedo and reflectivity but  for the 1 billion people or more that  live in the indo-gangetic and other  major basins of the himalaya  but the  point is here that the study of these  glaciers or other aspects of the  himalayan ecosystem is much lower than  what we would have liked  a recent ipcc report called the himalaya  a white spot because of the paucity of  research that exists on many areas so  this is an ecosystem that really  requires a lot more attention and a lot  more research
 

